gher  end  exxon em ccd cameras so that's the only  back illuminated sc mos camera that has  that type of  technology built into it and that helps  allow for the deepest cooling  and with these cameras they have a  higher dark  current so it's very important to  suppress that  dark current from being an issue with  those cameras and also  importantly reducing the amount of  spurious  hot pixels as well and for the kinds of  studies that have just been presented we  can see that it's very important  to reduce any of these spurious events  from  affecting our data  also importantly the these cameras have  extended dynamic range which allows them  to handle  the widest signal ranges so that means  that there's  less need for adjusting camera settings  so that whatever  whenever you're imaging you can tolerate  a wide  signal range and get optimum performance  from the  from the camera and since many  studies like the one that's been shown  that quantitative accuracy  is one of the fundamentals of any  imaging cameras whenever we're trying to  analyze that data  so beyond just the simple taking of the  images  as we can see it's very important to  have a quantitative camera  and that has been one of the ams and  these cameras as well to make them as  accurate as possible  so there's two sensor options available  with that sona  camera series and one of these uses the  gs 2020 bsi sensor  and the other uses the gs 400 bsi sensor  and that's the one the gs 400 bsi sensor  has been is the one that has been  discussed in this  work um and we can see just from  comparing the specs and these  the sona 4.2 b-6  which uses that 2020 bsi sensor  it um has a smaller pixel size  and but it's also low noise  and high speed so that's a very flexible  camera and it's the same sensor format  as the previous generation of scmos  cameras  so that's ideal for people looking to  upgrade  however the other camera that has  covered  the has this larger pixel size so  although it's still  4.